      <h2>Vendored GitHub Actions</h2>
      <p>In October 2020 I brought three community GitHub Actions into my own GitHub account and reviewed them, so my pipelines only run code I have read. Each one started life as an open-source community action, and the README of each copy gives the reason it moved: only run code you control and have gone over. It was an early supply-chain hygiene habit: a third-party action runs with your repository's secrets, so pointing your workflows at a copy you own and have read is cheap insurance against a tag being moved or an upstream being compromised.</p>

      <h2 id='docker-publish'>actions-docker-publish</h2>
      <p><a href='https://github.com/liskl/actions-docker-publish'>github.com/liskl/actions-docker-publish</a></p>
      <p>Builds a Docker image and pushes it, using the git branch as the image tag; the default branch publishes as <code>latest</code>. It works with Docker Hub, GitHub Packages, GitHub Container Registry or any custom registry through the <code>registry</code> input.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>snapshot</code> pushes an extra tag built from a timestamp and the short git sha</li>
        <li><code>tag_names</code> and <code>tag_semver</code> tag images from git tags, with <code>tag_semver</code> turning <code>v1.2.3</code> into <code>1.2.3</code>, <code>1.2</code> and <code>1</code></li>
        <li><code>cache</code>, <code>buildargs</code> and <code>buildoptions</code> tune the build</li>
        <li><code>dockerfile</code>, <code>workdir</code> and <code>context</code> pick what gets built and from where</li>
        <li>Outputs: <code>tag</code>, <code>snapshot-tag</code> and <code>digest</code></li>
      </ul>
      <p>Testing: bats unit tests run during the image build, with a mock shim standing in for <code>docker</code> and <code>date</code>. Separate integration workflows publish to a custom registry and to GitHub's package registry, a bump_version workflow tags releases, and a Renovate config is checked in for dependency updates.</p>
<pre><code>- name: Publish to Registry
  uses: liskl/actions-docker-publish@main
  with:
    name: myorg/myimage
    username: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_USERNAME }}
    password: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_PASSWORD }}
    snapshot: true</code></pre>

      <h2 id='github-tag'>actions-github-tag</h2>
      <p><a href='https://github.com/liskl/actions-github-tag'>github.com/liskl/actions-github-tag</a></p>
      <p>Bumps and tags the release branch with the next SemVer version on merge. Commit messages can ask for a <code>#major</code>, <code>#minor</code> or <code>#patch</code> bump; otherwise it falls back to <code>DEFAULT_BUMP</code> (<code>minor</code> unless set). Other options include <code>WITH_V</code> for a <code>v</code> prefix, <code>RELEASE_BRANCHES</code> for which branches cut release tags (<code>main</code> by default), and <code>DRY_RUN</code> to compute the next version without tagging. Any other branch gets a version suffixed with the short commit sha and no tag is pushed.</p>

      <h2 id='k8s-deployer'>actions-k8s-deployer</h2>
      <p><a href='https://github.com/liskl/actions-k8s-deployer'>github.com/liskl/actions-k8s-deployer</a></p>
      <p>Decodes a base64 <code>KUBE_CONFIG_DATA</code> secret into a kubeconfig and runs whatever <code>kubectl</code> command you pass it, which covers both the deploy and the check that it worked.</p>
<pre><code>- name: Deploy
  uses: liskl/actions-k8s-deployer@main
  env:
    KUBE_CONFIG_DATA: ${{ secrets.KUBE_CONFIG_DATA }}
  with:
    args: set image deployment/app app=registry.example.com/app:${{ github.sha }}

- name: Verify rollout
  uses: liskl/actions-k8s-deployer@main
  env:
    KUBE_CONFIG_DATA: ${{ secrets.KUBE_CONFIG_DATA }}
  with:
    args: rollout status deployment/app</code></pre>

      <h2 id='then-and-now'>Then and now</h2>
      <p>The same three jobs (tag, publish, deploy) still run this site, with the deploy step now pulled rather than pushed. Every merge to master is a release: conventional-commit tagging pushes a <code>vX.Y.Z</code> tag, CI builds the nginx image and pushes it to GitHub Container Registry, and Flux image automation picks up the new tag and rolls it out on the <a href='/server.html'>homelab cluster</a>.</p>

